<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · South America</p><h1 class="st-title">Moche</h1><p class="st-meta">100 CE – 800 CE (approx.) · capital Huacas de Moche</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A coastal civilisation of northern Peru that built huge adobe pyramids and produced the most lifelike portrait pottery and metalwork of the ancient Andes.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Moche, or Mochica, culture developed around 100 CE in the river valleys of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru's</a> north coast, especially the Moche and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chicama-valley">Chicama</a> valleys near modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trujillo-peru">Trujillo</a>. Local elites drew on earlier coastal traditions such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cupisnique">Cupisnique</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salinar">Salinar</a> and extended irrigation canals across the desert.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Moche society was ruled by warrior-priest lords. Its capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huacas-de-moche">Huacas de Moche</a> had the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huaca-del-sol">Huaca del Sol</a>, one of the largest adobe structures in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>, and the painted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huaca-de-la-luna">Huaca de la Luna</a>. The tombs of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lord-of-sipan">Lord of Sipán</a>, found in 1987, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lady-of-cao">Lady of Cao</a> show the wealth of gold, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> regalia. Moche pottery includes realistic portrait vessels and painted scenes of warfare, sacrifice and daily life.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Heavy <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/el-nino">El Niño</a> floods and droughts in the 6th century disrupted Moche society, and its centres shifted north to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pampa-grande">Pampa Grande</a> and Galindo before collapsing around 800 CE. Its traditions passed to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/sican">Sicán</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-chimor">Kingdom of Chimor</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/moche.jpg" alt="Moche" width="320" height="479"><figcaption>Image: QQuantum · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:MocheEN.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>100 CE – 800 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/2nd-century">2nd century</a> – <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>theocratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Huacas de Moche</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Lord of Sipán, Lady of Cao</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Moche religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Mochica</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/peru">Peru</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Mochica, Moche culture</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=450&amp;focus=moche" aria-label="Moche on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2686 2669 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Moche lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M2790 2675h0M2814 2737h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3006 2925h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M3006 2925h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Peru. <a href="/atlas?year=450&amp;focus=moche">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
